  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nPatchy fog is possible again this morning, though unlikely to\nnecessitate a special weather statement or dense fog advisory \nas temperatures are a touch warmer than last night, with less \ncoverage of patchy fog.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA 1036 mb high is centered over the mid-Atlantic region and will\nremain there as it dissipates to around 1028 mb by the end of\nthe week. Steady southwest flow on the backside of this high \nwill advect warmer weather to the region as we see highs in the \nmid to upper 50s today climb through the week and peak in the \nmid 60s by Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nLow pressure moves into the northern Great Lakes by Sunday with\nan attendant cold front moving east across the area. The low \npressure system will be in a weakening state as its parent \nupper-level trough lifts northeast and flattens, so while rain\nshowers are likely to accompany this system, showers will be\nlight with little QPF. Temperatures will noticeably drop into \nthe 40s next week.",
